WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.590
(audience cheering)
- Hi.

00:00:01.590 --> 00:00:03.242
- Oh my gosh. Hello.

00:00:03.242 --> 00:00:05.220
That was so beautiful, man.

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:07.230
That was, I love that.

00:00:07.230 --> 00:00:09.810
- Well I had real shoes
to fill so I was honored

00:00:09.810 --> 00:00:11.612
to get to do it with,
with you and for you.

00:00:11.612 --> 00:00:13.230
(audience applauding)

00:00:13.230 --> 00:00:14.190
- Oh my gosh.

00:00:14.190 --> 00:00:15.840
Well it was, you sound incredible,

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:16.673
you and my band.

00:00:16.673 --> 00:00:18.450
I was like, "Oh, I could
listen to this all day."

00:00:18.450 --> 00:00:21.840
That whole vibe is so
perfect for all of y'all.

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:23.910
But thank you for stepping
in for me with that song.

00:00:23.910 --> 00:00:25.440
Did you sing Christmas music growing up?

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:26.520
You a big fan?

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:28.950
- Yeah, we grew up without
television, without radio,

00:00:28.950 --> 00:00:31.320
and so singing was like
our main tradition.

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:34.560
So tons of Christmas songs and folk songs,

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:37.290
and Swiss German songs,
all kinds for the holidays.

00:00:37.290 --> 00:00:38.820
- Oh, that's so fun.

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:40.560
Well, you also sang, I love this,

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:42.373
we just had Dolly on, I love Dolly Parton.

00:00:42.373 --> 00:00:44.488
You've recently sang for her, right?

00:00:44.488 --> 00:00:45.321
(audience cheering)
- Yeah. I love...

00:00:45.321 --> 00:00:46.473
Isn't Dolly the best?

00:00:47.370 --> 00:00:48.692
Yeah, I was really honored.

00:00:48.692 --> 00:00:52.920
Lauren Sanchez and Jeff Bezos
had me come and get to sing

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:56.831
for her as she was awarded that
$100 million civility grant.

00:00:56.831 --> 00:00:58.320
It was incredible.

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:01.230
And I actually got to sing
"Jolene" for her there,

00:01:01.230 --> 00:01:04.005
which made me very nervous,
but it was very fun, yeah.

00:01:04.005 --> 00:01:06.030
(audience applauding)

00:01:06.030 --> 00:01:08.460
- You know what, I'll tell
you what, I've had to sing one

00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:09.870
of her songs in front of her

00:01:09.870 --> 00:01:12.000
and it is terrifying,

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:13.140
'cause, you know what I'm saying?

00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:15.270
The person is there, that's their baby.

00:01:15.270 --> 00:01:16.500
They made it iconic.

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:18.270
You're like, "What am I doing?"

00:01:18.270 --> 00:01:19.680
Like it is terrifying.

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:22.807
Even if you feel like a
confident singer, it's like,

00:01:22.807 --> 00:01:24.570
"Man don't screw this up."

00:01:24.570 --> 00:01:27.420
- Yeah. I had like the sweat
mustache, the sweaty boobs

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:28.320
the whole nine yards.

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:30.560
You guys know what I'm talking about.

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:31.393
(Kelly laughing)
It's real.

00:01:31.393 --> 00:01:34.200
- I have like, the sweat
trickles down the back.

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:35.550
- That happens.
- It's ugly, yeah.

00:01:35.550 --> 00:01:38.214
- So, do you remember the
first time you met Dolly

00:01:38.214 --> 00:01:40.140
or maybe one of your favorite
experiences with her?

00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:42.000
Because you've worked
with her a few times.

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:44.430
- Yes. I grew up on a
homestead with an outhouse.

00:01:44.430 --> 00:01:46.826
So her, for me growing up was like a hero

00:01:46.826 --> 00:01:49.380
because she had a somewhat
similar lifestyle.

00:01:49.380 --> 00:01:51.930
So, I was really excited.

00:01:51.930 --> 00:01:53.640
I had her sing on a song of mine

00:01:53.640 --> 00:01:55.230
called "My Father's Daughter."

00:01:55.230 --> 00:01:56.280
She came in and sang.

00:01:56.280 --> 00:01:57.270
She showed up like that.

00:01:57.270 --> 00:01:58.951
That was 7:45 AM.

00:01:58.951 --> 00:01:59.784
(audience laughs)

00:01:59.784 --> 00:02:02.490
She looks on point at all times.

00:02:02.490 --> 00:02:03.323
She put me at ease.

00:02:03.323 --> 00:02:04.830
She's like, "Now, hey,
I'm so glad that I'm here

00:02:04.830 --> 00:02:06.960
but I need you to keep me honest
in case I don't sing good.

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:08.250
And in case you're too intimidated.

00:02:08.250 --> 00:02:09.510
I brought it, gotta keep me honest."

00:02:09.510 --> 00:02:10.817
And I was like, "All right."

00:02:12.756 --> 00:02:13.589
(Kelly laughing)
It was amazing.

00:02:13.589 --> 00:02:14.943
- I love your impression.

00:02:16.849 --> 00:02:17.760
I I love her.

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:19.380
I thought, and I thought that was so cool

00:02:19.380 --> 00:02:21.420
that Jeff actually chose her

00:02:21.420 --> 00:02:23.640
for that grant because,
I mean, her whole career

00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:25.260
she's done amazing things musically

00:02:25.260 --> 00:02:27.660
obviously, but as a human,

00:02:27.660 --> 00:02:29.700
she's done incredible things for educators

00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:31.890
like reading, for just, so many things.

00:02:31.890 --> 00:02:34.290
She's just a person
that's constantly giving.

00:02:34.290 --> 00:02:36.120
So, it was like the perfect person

00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:38.850
to grant that money to do good with.

00:02:38.850 --> 00:02:40.937
I was very stoked about that.

00:02:40.937 --> 00:02:43.890
But you also recently
took a trip to Italy,

00:02:43.890 --> 00:02:45.600
which, I love Italy.

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:48.390
So, you ate breakfast and where was this?

00:02:48.390 --> 00:02:49.740
- This was in, let's see,

00:02:49.740 --> 00:02:52.020
where was I, in Venice at the Iman Hotel.

00:02:52.020 --> 00:02:54.870
My son and I, I had some work,
I had some shows over there,

00:02:54.870 --> 00:02:56.940
but my son and I made a vacation of it,

00:02:56.940 --> 00:02:58.890
and this was in this incredible place.

00:02:58.890 --> 00:03:00.180
So, like painted ceilings.

00:03:00.180 --> 00:03:01.743
It's not like Alaska, y'all.

00:03:02.970 --> 00:03:04.870
There was toilet paper and everything.

00:03:05.965 --> 00:03:07.200
(audience laughs)
It was nice.

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:08.880
- It was fancy.

00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:11.580
I took a trip to Italy once

00:03:11.580 --> 00:03:12.413
and I actually went to Venice.

00:03:12.413 --> 00:03:15.390
And it's cool to go on the
steps, like on the thing.

00:03:15.390 --> 00:03:19.260
Did you see, I ended up seeing
like Vivaldi Four Seasons,

00:03:19.260 --> 00:03:21.000
and they dressed up like in character.

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:22.830
You went in the room and sat in the seats

00:03:22.830 --> 00:03:24.810
and listened like as if
it was back in the day.

00:03:24.810 --> 00:03:26.100
Did you do anything like that?

00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:28.320
- Okay, that sounds way
better than what I did.

00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:29.673
I ate a gelato.

00:03:31.230 --> 00:03:33.450
But yours sounds like it was winning.

00:03:33.450 --> 00:03:35.310
- I was like, well I think, I didn't know

00:03:35.310 --> 00:03:38.040
if I'd ever go back, 'cause I
also did feel claustrophobic,

00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:40.500
'cause the water at night,
I was like, "Am I drowning?"

00:03:40.500 --> 00:03:42.450
So I don't know if I
thought I'd ever go back

00:03:42.450 --> 00:03:44.670
'cause I was like, I need to
do it all like really quickly.

00:03:44.670 --> 00:03:46.743
But I definitely had gelato too, Girl.

00:03:47.580 --> 00:03:52.020
So is it true that your grandfather
was actually from Italy?

00:03:52.020 --> 00:03:55.500
- Switzerland, and he believed
that Europe was gonna fail.

00:03:55.500 --> 00:03:58.200
He thought that if Europe had
a certain population index

00:03:58.200 --> 00:03:59.790
the whole civilization would fail.

00:03:59.790 --> 00:04:02.490
He talked artists, poets, philosophers

00:04:02.490 --> 00:04:05.220
and painters into thinking
maybe this was true.

00:04:05.220 --> 00:04:09.128
They all decided to leave Europe
and form like a free place

00:04:09.128 --> 00:04:11.460
in Alaska where they were
giving away free land

00:04:11.460 --> 00:04:13.950
if he would go up there for
a whole winter and not die.

00:04:13.950 --> 00:04:15.930
So he went ahead of them on a boat

00:04:15.930 --> 00:04:18.815
and he stood away on an Italian
ship and was discovered.

00:04:18.815 --> 00:04:21.930
But my granddad's specialty
was learning folk songs

00:04:21.930 --> 00:04:23.970
in the dialects of different villages.

00:04:23.970 --> 00:04:27.420
And so he happened to
recognize the captain's dialect

00:04:27.420 --> 00:04:29.460
and guessed the village
he was from in Italy

00:04:29.460 --> 00:04:32.130
and he happened to be right
and they gave him safe passage

00:04:32.130 --> 00:04:33.810
and that's how I ended up in Alaska.

00:04:33.810 --> 00:04:35.977
Yeah, crazy right?

00:04:35.977 --> 00:04:37.410
- What?
(audience applauding)

00:04:37.410 --> 00:04:40.380
That's a really cool story.

00:04:40.380 --> 00:04:41.310
That's an amazing story

00:04:41.310 --> 00:04:43.830
of how your family navigated
their way and ended up

00:04:43.830 --> 00:04:46.740
in Alaska, which my brother
actually lived there

00:04:46.740 --> 00:04:51.060
for like seven years and it,
that is hard to do, like,

00:04:51.060 --> 00:04:53.820
'cause of the sun is just gone for, like,

00:04:53.820 --> 00:04:56.370
a long period of time, yeah.

00:04:56.370 --> 00:04:57.203
- It's true.

00:04:57.203 --> 00:05:00.090
- It's a lot different than
Switzerland, I feel like.

00:05:00.090 --> 00:05:03.090
So, Jewel, how have the
holidays been for you so far?

00:05:03.090 --> 00:05:04.440
Like how has Thanksgiving?

00:05:04.440 --> 00:05:05.273
- It was good.

00:05:05.273 --> 00:05:06.330
I'm a single mom, as you know,

00:05:06.330 --> 00:05:08.520
so single parenting is a big adjustment.

00:05:08.520 --> 00:05:10.320
I've been divorced for seven years.

00:05:10.320 --> 00:05:12.692
So, my ex keeps my son for Thanksgiving

00:05:12.692 --> 00:05:15.330
and I have him for Christmas
and it's really great

00:05:15.330 --> 00:05:17.460
but it's a trip to get used to being alone

00:05:17.460 --> 00:05:20.220
for a holiday like that and
all my family in Alaska.

00:05:20.220 --> 00:05:21.090
But it wasn't too bad.

00:05:21.090 --> 00:05:22.800
I didn't see a tiny violin.

00:05:22.800 --> 00:05:25.268
It was like (imitates violins)
brought the show down.

00:05:25.268 --> 00:05:28.140
- No! It's so funny.

00:05:28.140 --> 00:05:29.280
I'm gonna turn it around.

00:05:29.280 --> 00:05:32.190
Like I, my ex actually has my kids

00:05:32.190 --> 00:05:33.791
every Thanksgiving as well.

00:05:33.791 --> 00:05:36.202
It's not like my favorite holiday.

00:05:36.202 --> 00:05:37.890
And it is for his family.

00:05:37.890 --> 00:05:39.180
So I was like, "Cool."

00:05:39.180 --> 00:05:42.240
And I actually, it was weird for me.

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:43.710
I don't know if it was, I
know you've been, you know,

00:05:43.710 --> 00:05:45.690
divorced for a while but it was so weird

00:05:45.690 --> 00:05:47.730
'cause you've always had a kid there,

00:05:47.730 --> 00:05:49.950
and then all of a sudden
you're alone and you're, like,

00:05:49.950 --> 00:05:53.553
at first it's weird and then
you're kind of, like, okay,

00:05:54.698 --> 00:05:58.440
you're like, I can do things.

00:05:58.440 --> 00:06:00.840
Like, it's kind of incredible.

00:06:00.840 --> 00:06:02.850
You do get a little me time.

00:06:02.850 --> 00:06:03.683
- Yeah. I feel

00:06:03.683 --> 00:06:06.000
like it must be good preparation
for empty nest syndrome.

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:07.770
It's like, it forces me to invest

00:06:07.770 --> 00:06:10.530
and like I have to do
things before I had a child.

00:06:10.530 --> 00:06:12.270
I suddenly seem to forget what I did,

00:06:12.270 --> 00:06:13.231
suddenly when I'm alone
I'm like, "What did I do?

00:06:13.231 --> 00:06:15.990
I did things."

00:06:15.990 --> 00:06:16.823
But you get better at it.

00:06:16.823 --> 00:06:18.057
- I know, I know.

00:06:18.057 --> 00:06:20.760
But it is, in the beginning
you're just, I was like a mess.

00:06:20.760 --> 00:06:23.070
But now I'm like, it's awesome, you know?

00:06:23.070 --> 00:06:24.390
And then you have you, you know,

00:06:24.390 --> 00:06:26.580
absence does make the heart fonder.

00:06:26.580 --> 00:06:28.950
So, you're spearheading, actually,

00:06:28.950 --> 00:06:31.470
something called the #notAloneChallenge.

00:06:31.470 --> 00:06:33.270
So, tell us about that.

00:06:33.270 --> 00:06:35.190
- Yeah, so I moved out at 15.

00:06:35.190 --> 00:06:37.110
I knew statistically kids like me end

00:06:37.110 --> 00:06:39.720
up repeating the cycle and I
didn't wanna be a statistic.

00:06:39.720 --> 00:06:42.510
I grew up in a family where
abuse had been generational

00:06:42.510 --> 00:06:45.630
and so I wanted to see if
happiness was a learnable skill.

00:06:45.630 --> 00:06:48.270
So I learned a lot of skills
for myself that really

00:06:48.270 --> 00:06:51.360
helped me learn what I call
a new emotional language

00:06:51.360 --> 00:06:53.370
because not everybody
has access to therapy.

00:06:53.370 --> 00:06:54.390
In fact, 50%

00:06:54.390 --> 00:06:56.820
of the people that need mental
health help don't have access

00:06:56.820 --> 00:06:57.900
to any tools.

00:06:57.900 --> 00:06:59.310
And happiness shouldn't be elite.

00:06:59.310 --> 00:07:00.143
It shouldn't be just

00:07:00.143 --> 00:07:01.800
for the people that can afford something

00:07:01.800 --> 00:07:03.390
or afford a therapist.

00:07:03.390 --> 00:07:06.000
So the #notAloneChallenge is bringing

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:08.070
proven tools that can scale

00:07:08.070 --> 00:07:11.473
to everybody that lives in
these mental health deserts.

00:07:11.473 --> 00:07:12.532
(audience applauds)

00:07:12.532 --> 00:07:13.770
- I love, that is so important.

00:07:13.770 --> 00:07:17.820
And it's also, like, not only
that people can't afford it.

00:07:17.820 --> 00:07:21.720
I know people like from
where I'm from in the south

00:07:21.720 --> 00:07:23.610
it's still kind of looked down-upon

00:07:23.610 --> 00:07:26.280
and people get shamed for it,

00:07:26.280 --> 00:07:29.238
which is such a crazy
thing to me, for lack

00:07:29.238 --> 00:07:33.090
of a better word because
it's one of those things

00:07:33.090 --> 00:07:35.280
it's nice to have somebody
outside your circle

00:07:35.280 --> 00:07:38.370
that literally is trained in
ways to help you navigate,

00:07:38.370 --> 00:07:41.767
give you tools with whatever
you're dealing with.

00:07:41.767 --> 00:07:44.700
It's like, if you need
help with your car, you go

00:07:44.700 --> 00:07:46.800
to a mechanic, if you need
help, you know what I'm saying?

00:07:46.800 --> 00:07:49.890
If you, it just seems
so natural to me, but I,

00:07:49.890 --> 00:07:52.110
it's not only, it's, it's
amazing 'cause the money,

00:07:52.110 --> 00:07:55.320
but also I think society
needs to really change

00:07:55.320 --> 00:07:57.510
and evolve in the sense

00:07:57.510 --> 00:08:00.585
of quit shaming people for
it or looking down-upon it.

00:08:00.585 --> 00:08:03.420
But how, how could people get involved?

00:08:03.420 --> 00:08:04.560
- Oh, if you wanna get involved,

00:08:04.560 --> 00:08:07.230
you go to notalonechallenge.org,

00:08:07.230 --> 00:08:09.810
and there you'll find a lot of
mental health tools for free.

00:08:09.810 --> 00:08:12.840
You'll find a ton of resources,
and then you can also

00:08:12.840 --> 00:08:14.340
find all of our auction items there.

00:08:14.340 --> 00:08:16.020
And we have incredible auction items.

00:08:16.020 --> 00:08:18.300
We're raising money to
be able to scale proven

00:08:18.300 --> 00:08:21.427
behavioral tools so that
everybody has access to them.

00:08:21.427 --> 00:08:22.775
(audience applauds)

00:08:22.775 --> 00:08:25.173
- That's incredible,
Jewel. That's so cool.

00:08:26.760 --> 00:08:29.130
So I hear that there's
an auction and, like,

00:08:29.130 --> 00:08:31.410
even, like, tickets, from sports tickets

00:08:31.410 --> 00:08:33.270
to like Billy Eilish tickets.

00:08:33.270 --> 00:08:35.640
You're offering something
cool as well, right?

00:08:35.640 --> 00:08:38.160
- Yeah, I thought that I would
write a personalized song,

00:08:38.160 --> 00:08:40.350
so people can bid on a
song that I'll write.

00:08:40.350 --> 00:08:41.280
I'll write about your dog

00:08:41.280 --> 00:08:42.630
if that's what you want
me to write it about.

00:08:42.630 --> 00:08:45.985
I'll write about your mama,
I'll write about your son.

00:08:45.985 --> 00:08:49.382
But I thought that'd be fun
so people can bid on that.

00:08:49.382 --> 00:08:51.270
- I love, that's such a cool thing.

00:08:51.270 --> 00:08:53.790
It's such a personal
thing to do for someone.

00:08:53.790 --> 00:08:54.903
It's really cool.

